course: biology
lesson: 10
title: Rivers, lakes and ponds
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Fresh water is a habitat of its own: rivers rush and carry mud and stones, while ponds and lakes stand still and fill with plants, insects, frogs and fish, and each kind of water has its own crowd.
explain: A pond is a jungle in miniature: at a pond-dipping tray you can watch a dragonfly larva shoot out its hinged jaw and grab a tadpole, while a pond skater walks on the water's stretchy skin above.
explain: Winter does not kill a lake, because water is heaviest at 4 degrees: the coldest water and the ice float on top like a lid, and fish swim on in the liquid water below.
explain: Cartoon piranhas turn a cow into a skeleton in seconds; real red-bellied piranhas mostly eat insects, fish, worms and plants, feed in groups mainly for safety, and attacks on people are rare and usually just a bite.
explain: Cartoons often show river otters floating on their backs and smashing shells on a stone on their tummy; that trick belongs to sea otters, which also hold paws while they sleep so they do not drift apart.
fact: Pond skaters are insects with 6 legs; they stand on the water's surface, which acts like a thin stretchy skin, on long legs with water-repelling hairs, and they feel the ripples of insects falling in.
fact: The kingfisher dives head-first from a perch and its dagger beak enters the water with little splash; Japanese engineers shaped a bullet train's nose like it to make the train quieter in tunnels.
fact: Atlantic salmon grow big at sea, then swim up the river where they hatched to lay their eggs, finding it by smell; they stop eating on the way and live on the fat built up at sea, and they can leap over 3 metres to climb waterfalls.
fact: Otters close their ears and nostrils underwater, keep warm with thick, air-trapping fur, and sea otters, not river otters, crack shells with stones while floating on their backs.
fact: Dragonfly larvae live underwater for up to several years and catch insects, tadpoles and small fish with a hinged jaw that shoots out, and adult dragonflies catch over 90 per cent of the insects they chase.
fact: Grey herons stand still in shallow water so fish do not notice them, then stab with a sudden strike, and they also raid garden ponds for goldfish.
fact: Beavers fell trees with their teeth and build dams to make deep ponds that protect their lodge and do not freeze to the bottom; their orange front teeth contain iron, which makes them extra hard, and never stop growing.
fact: Water is heaviest at about 4 degrees Celsius, so it sinks to the bottom of a lake while colder water and ice float on top, and fish survive under the ice.
fact: Red-bellied piranhas have razor-sharp triangular teeth but mostly eat insects, fish, worms and plants, and attacks on people are rare, usually just bites when water is low and food is scarce.
fact: After heavy rain, water rushing off the land makes rivers faster, higher and brown with soil, and the Amazon carries more water than the next several biggest rivers put together.
fact: Great crested newts live on land for much of the year, wrap each egg in a folded leaf in a pond in spring, are protected by law in the UK and do best in ponds without fish, because fish eat their eggs and young.
fact: Freshwater mussels filter and clean river water as they feed, pearl mussels can live over 100 years, and their babies spend their first months on the gills of salmon or trout.
fact: A dragonfly has 6 legs and 4 wings, like many insects.
exercise: How can a pond skater stand on water? | The water's surface acts like a stretchy skin, and its waxy hairs do not break it | It is so light that it floats on the top like a dry leaf, and it has air inside its body to help | It runs so fast it never sinks | It stands on tiny bubbles it blows
exercise: How do pond skaters find food? | They feel the ripples of insects falling onto the water | They dive down to the muddy bottom of the pond to catch worms | They eat the pond weed | They wait for the heron to drop scraps
exercise: How does a kingfisher catch a fish? | It dives head-first from a perch and grabs it in its beak | It scoops it up with its feet while flying low over the river | It waits for fish to jump into its beak | It stuns fish with a loud call
exercise: What did Japanese engineers copy from the kingfisher? | The shape of its beak, for a quieter bullet train nose | Its bright colours, for a paint that makes trains go faster | Its feathers, for raincoats | Its eyes, for underwater cameras
exercise: Why do salmon leap up waterfalls? | They are swimming up to the stream where they hatched to lay eggs | They are escaping from the sea for the winter | They are chasing the flies that buzz above the top of the waterfall | They are trying to reach the sea
exercise: How do salmon find their home river? | Mostly by its smell | By following the stars | By listening for waterfalls | By following big ships
exercise: Which otters crack shells on their tummies while floating on their backs? | Sea otters | River otters | All otters in every river | None, it only happens in cartoons
exercise: How does a dragonfly larva catch a tadpole? | Its hinged jaw shoots out like an arm and grabs it | It stings it with the sharp poisonous tip of its tail | It spins a web under water | It sucks it in like a vacuum cleaner
exercise: How good are adult dragonflies at catching insects? | They catch over 90 per cent of those they chase | They catch about one in ten of the insects they chase | They never catch flying insects | They catch only insects that are asleep
exercise: Why does a heron stand so still at the edge of the lake? | So fish do not notice it before it strikes | It is fast asleep, standing there on one leg | It is frozen stiff by the icy cold water of the river | It is waiting for the fish to fall asleep so it can pick them up
exercise: Why do beavers build dams? | To make a deep pond that protects their lodge and does not freeze to the bottom | To catch fish trapped behind the dam | To stop other beavers crossing their river and stealing the trees they have cut down for food | To store logs to eat in summer
exercise: Why are a beaver's front teeth orange? | They contain iron, which makes them extra strong | They are stained by the orange bark it chews all day | The beaver is ill | They are rusty from the water
exercise: Why do fish survive under a frozen lake? | The ice floats on top like a lid, and the water below stays liquid | Fish freeze solid in winter and then thaw out again in spring | The fish swim to the sea before winter | Fish make their own heat like mammals, which melts the ice around them
exercise: What do red-bellied piranhas mostly eat? | Insects, fish, worms and plants | Cows and people that fall into the river | Only the blood of other wounded animals | Only other piranhas smaller than themselves
exercise: Why does a river run muddy brown after heavy rain? | Fast water rushing off the land carries soil into it | Rain is brown in heavy storms | Fish stir up the mud to feed on the worms hiding in the river bed | The river bed melts in the rain
exercise: Where do great crested newts lay their eggs? | In ponds, each egg wrapped in a folded leaf | In holes in dry sand | On the backs of frogs | In nests of mud on the bank, away from the water and the fish
exercise: How do freshwater mussels help keep rivers clear? | They filter the water as they feed | They eat all the small fish in the river | They pump all the mud out of the river | They block the river with their empty shells
exercise: Where do baby freshwater pearl mussels spend their first months? | On the gills of salmon or trout | Inside a kingfisher's nest in the bank | Floating freely on the surface of the water | Safely inside their mother's shell
test: «piranhas eat a whole cow in like 5 seconds, seen it in a cartoon» What is right? | A myth: they mostly eat insects, fish, worms and plants, and attacks are rare | True: they hunt cows every day | True, but only in the zoo | A myth: piranhas have no teeth at all, so they could never bite through a cow's skin
test: «cartoon otter cracking shells on its tummy in the river, cute» Which is right? | That is a sea otter trick; river otters do not do it | All otters do it, in rivers and in the sea | No otter ever uses stones | Only beavers do that
test: What would happen to the fish in a lake if ice sank instead of floating? | The lake would freeze from the bottom up, and many fish could not survive | Nothing, fish like ice | The fish would live on top of the sunken ice | The lake would slowly turn into a river and flow away down to the sea
test: Count them: four pond skaters and two dragonflies on the pond. How many legs? | 36 | 24 | 30 | 40
test: Count them: three dragonflies zip past. How many wings? | 12 | 6 | 9 | 18
test: The school pond is going to have goldfish put in it, and great crested newts live there. What is the cleverest worry? | Fish eat newt eggs and larvae, so the newts may disappear | The goldfish will all be eaten by the hungry newts | The pond will freeze faster | The newts will turn orange
test: «shut up, the salmon don't eat anything going up the river? how'd they jump then» Which is the best answer? | They live on the fat and strength they built up at sea | They eat waterfall rocks | They eat flies while jumping high out of the water | They take food from fishermen
test: «beaver's teeth'd get worn down to nothing chewing trees» What is right? | They never stop growing, and the iron makes them extra strong | Beavers grow new teeth every week | Beavers use their claws, not teeth | Yes, old beavers have no teeth left and must eat soft water plants
test: «found a newt, gonna take it home to me own pond» What is right in the UK? | Great crested newts are protected; you must not move them without a licence | Fine, newts like being moved | Fine, as long as you give it a jar | Only bad if it is winter
test: «dragonfly larva got my tadpole, bloody vicious, kill it» Which is the best answer? | It is a normal pond hunter, part of the pond's food chain | It is a pest that should be wiped out | It is a baby fish gone wrong | It only eats when it is ill or very hungry after a long, cold winter
test: «muddy river after the storm, did someone dump paint in it» Which is the best answer? | Rain rushing off the land carries soil and makes the river faster and browner | Yes, the colour is always pollution | Fish turned the water brown | The mud rises from the riverbed by itself on rainy days, as the river bed soaks up rain
test: «a mussel 100 years old? older than my great-gran» What is right? | Freshwater pearl mussels can live over 100 years | No mussel lives more than a year | Only sea mussels live that long, never river ones | They live for ever
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the survival of fish beneath the ice of a frozen lake? | Water attains its greatest density at approximately four degrees Celsius, whereby colder water and ice remain at the surface and the water beneath remains liquid | The fish freeze entirely and are revived in spring | The fish migrate to the sea prior to the onset of winter | The fish generate sufficient bodily heat to melt the ice
exam: Identify the principal purpose of the dam constructed by the beaver. | The creation of a deep pond which protects the entrance to the lodge and does not freeze to the bottom | The capture of fish confined behind the dam | The prevention of passage by other beavers | The storage of timber for consumption in summer, when the bark of standing trees has become too hard to digest
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the diet of red-bellied piranhas. | Red-bellied piranhas subsist principally upon insects, fish, worms and plants, attacks upon humans being infrequent | Red-bellied piranhas subsist principally upon cattle and humans which enter the water, devouring them in a matter of seconds | Red-bellied piranhas subsist exclusively upon blood | Red-bellied piranhas subsist exclusively upon other piranhas
exam: Ascertain the aggregate number of legs possessed by five pond skaters and one dragonfly. | Thirty-six | Thirty | Twenty-four | Forty-two
